{
  "fixture": "de-herstellungskosten",
  "description": "DE-Pack: production cost under § 255 Abs. 2 HGB. The core adds components up; this table is the pack's, and it is the whole difference between one jurisdiction and the next. Germany requires material and production cost, their overhead, and the production-related depreciation of fixed assets; it leaves general administration, voluntary social benefits, occupational pensions and (per § 255 Abs. 3) borrowing costs to the preparer; and it forbids research and distribution outright. Here the preparer takes the administration option: 114,000.00 of mandatory components plus 12,000.00 of administration = 126,000.00, while 6,000.00 of distribution stays out because no election can lift a statutory prohibition. Note the four `defineDimensionValue` calls: cost centres are the tenant's own master data, not the pack's — \"Materialstelle\" is a fact about one company and not about German law — and until now they could only be declared through the in-memory construction path, which left every pack-built tenant with an empty registry and cost accounting unreachable on every shipped pack. Read this fixture next to `us-inventory-costing`, which drives the SAME books through the US pack and comes out 12,000.00 lower — general and administrative expense is a period charge under ASC 330, and that single row of pack data is the entire reason the inventory value differs.",
